fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Lauren Boebert (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Lauren Boebert seeks reelection in a Colorado district that leans Republican by about nine points, a structural advantage even as the national environment favors Democrats. Most race raters place the seat in the Likely Republican column, though The Economist, Split Ticket, and RealClearPolitics call it a Tossup, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a three-in-ten chance.\n\nDemocrat Eileen Laubacher has built an unusual financial edge for this seat, raising about $10.3 million to Boebert's $1.0 million and holding $3.6 million on hand against roughly $250,000. Late-September reports alleging improper conduct by Boebert, followed by a temporary protection order she obtained, have added uncertainty that no public polling has yet tested. The race remains Boebert's to lose, but the money gap and the news cycle give Democrats a genuine, if secondary, path.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1824, "margin": -5.77}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3343, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1945, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2743, "margin": -4.7}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2626, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.23, "margin": -4.7}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2582, "margin": -4.8}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2829, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2916, "margin": -4.0}]}